---
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
import RiderDayDemo from '../../components/visuals/RiderDayDemo.astro';
import '../../styles/details.css';
---
<ProjectLayout
  title="Is the Algorithm Being Fair to Riders?"
  description="Analysing 15,000 delivery records to separate rider skill from circumstance, and building a fairer score."
  question="Can a performance score be mathematically accurate and still be unfair?"
  intro="I reverse-engineered rider scoring across 15,000 delivery records to understand how traffic, weather, and rider experience were shaping performance scores, then built a condition-adjusted Fair Score to separate performance from circumstance."
  did={[
    { title: 'Reverse-engineered the scoring logic', body: 'Worked backwards from the existing score to understand what was driving it.' },
    { title: 'Separated skill from circumstance', body: 'Tested which factors reflected rider performance and which were largely outside the rider’s control.' },
    { title: 'Built a fairer alternative', body: 'Designed an adjusted scoring approach that reduced penalties for circumstances riders could not influence.' },
  ]}
  stoodOut="Riders with similar underlying performance could still receive meaningfully different scores because of the conditions around them."
  keyLine="A model can be consistent without necessarily being fair."
  proof={['15,000 records', '99.9% model fit', 'Fair Score framework']}
  reflection="This made me more interested in the difference between predicting performance and judging it fairly."
  detailsLabel="How I tested it"
  next={{ href: '/projects/healthcare-expenditure/', label: 'Next: Healthcare Expenditure' }}
>
  <section slot="visual" class="rf-try is-wide" id="try" aria-labelledby="rf-try-title">
    <div class="rf-why">
      <p class="rf-kicker">Why I made this</p>
      <h2 class="rf-why-title" id="rf-try-title">A score sees the outcome, not the day.</h2>
      <p>Delivery riders are often judged by a single number. That number knows how long a delivery took. It doesn’t know about the traffic jam, or the rain that started halfway through the shift.</p>
      <p>So I wanted to check something simple: <strong>is the score measuring the rider, or the conditions the rider happened to be in?</strong></p>
      <p class="rf-hint hand">try it before reading the rest ↓</p>
    </div>
    <RiderDayDemo />
    <p class="rf-after">That is the question the project tests at scale: across 15,000 delivery records, how much of the score was really the rider, and how much was the road. <a href="#how">See how I tested it ↓</a></p>
  </section>

  <div slot="details" class="xd-grid">
    <div class="xd-block">
      <h3>Score reconstruction</h3>
      <p>Treated the existing performance score as the thing to explain, and worked backwards to find which recorded factors reproduced it.</p>
      <p>The reconstructed model fit the original scores at 99.9%, which meant the logic was understood well enough to question it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="xd-block">
      <h3>Regression</h3>
      <p>Used regression to estimate how much each factor moved the score while holding the others constant.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="xd-block">
      <h3>Feature effects</h3>
      <p>Compared the size of each factor’s effect to see which ones were doing most of the work in the final number.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="xd-block">
      <h3>Controllable vs uncontrollable</h3>
      <p>Sorted the factors by whether a rider could reasonably influence them.</p>
      <div class="xd-split">
        <div><span class="lbl">largely outside the rider’s control</span>traffic · weather</div>
        <div><span class="lbl">closer to rider performance</span>what remains once circumstance is accounted for</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="xd-block">
      <h3>Limitations</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Where a factor sits between “controllable” and “not” is partly a judgement call.</li>
        <li>The records describe the conditions that were captured, not everything that happens on a delivery.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</ProjectLayout>

<style>
  .rf-try { scroll-margin-top: 96px; display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.75fr) minmax(0, 1.6fr); gap: clamp(28px, 4vw, 64px); align-items: start; }
  .rf-kicker { font-size: 0.72rem; letter-spacing: 0.16em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--cobalt); font-weight: 600; }
  .rf-why-title { margin-top: 10px; font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 1.2rem + 0.9vw, 2rem); line-height: 1.15; }
  .rf-why p:not(.rf-kicker) { margin-top: 14px; font-size: 0.98rem; line-height: 1.6; color: #4a4741; }
  .rf-why strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
  .rf-why .rf-hint { margin-top: 20px; font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--cobalt); transform: rotate(-2deg); width: fit-content; }
  .rf-after { grid-column: 2; margin-top: -8px; max-width: 60ch; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: #4a4741; }
  .rf-after a { color: var(--cobalt); font-weight: 500; border-bottom: 1.5px solid var(--yellow); white-space: nowrap; }
  @media (max-width: 1024px) {
    .rf-try { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .rf-after { grid-column: 1; margin-top: 0; }
    .rf-why p:not(.rf-kicker) { max-width: 60ch; }
  }
</style>
